  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1056 PM CST Sat Nov 14 2026\n\nTonight through Sunday...\n\nWinds remained light and southeasterly this evening, as temperatures \ndipped into the 30s and upper 20s. Overnight and into Sunday, a low \npressure system will move through the OK and TX panhandles, bringing \nshowers to parts of the south-central Plains. However, any rain will \nlikely remain well to the south and west of our forecast area. \nPartly cloudy skies will give way to sunshine by Sunday afternoon, \nas highs creep back into the upper 40s and low 50s. \n\nMonday through Wednesday... \n\nDry weather will continue to prevail through mid week, with the help \nof a ridge building through the central CONUS. High temperatures are \nforecast to climb a few degrees each day, reaching the lower to mid \n60s by Wednesday. Overnight, lows may dip into the low 30s along and \neast of the Missouri River Sunday night. Otherwise, expect morning \nlows to generally bottom out in the upper 30s to mid 40s. \n\nThe back edge of the ridge approaches Tuesday and Wednesday, \ntightening the pressure gradient, and causing southwesterly winds to \nbecome gusty. Guidance suggests speeds up to 30-40+ mph will be \nlikely at times. While this could increase fire danger concerns in \nunharvested areas, increasing RH ahead of an approaching shortwave \nshould help mitigate some issues. \n\nThe upper level shortwave will move through Wednesday evening and \novernight, pushing a cold front through the forecast area. Model \nsolutions continue to trend drier across our CWA with the frontal \npassage, leaving the better chance for precipitation to our east. \n\nThursday and Beyond...\n\nAnother trough will move into the western CONUS Thursday, with \nsurface low pressure developing east of the Rockies. Increasing warm \nair and moisture advection ahead of the low is expected to lead to \nat least some spotty showers by Friday and increasing precipitation \nchances through the weekend. \n\nThe actual surface low and trough then look to start passing through \nFriday night into Saturday, bringing widespread showers and perhaps \na few thunderstorms to the area (80-90% chance of precip). There \ncontinues to be some decent spread in timing and track of the system \nwhich will significantly impact precip duration and potentially \ntype, as there will be snow on the northwest side of the low. \nOverall, the majority of ensemble guidance continues to suggest we \nwill see all rain, with only a small chance (10-20%) of snow \nclipping far northeast Nebraska.",
